{"iter":1,"slug":"prose-approach-broad","ts":"2026-05-15T07:23:46Z","status":"kept","score":0.903226,"n":62,"delta":0.306452,"hypothesis":"64 word-situation examples in which the user asks how to *approach* a","dataset":{"path":"datasets/prose-approach-broad.jsonl","size":64},"training":{"adapter":"cap-prose-approach-broad","final_loss":1.5766,"elapsed_s":185},"asi":{"what_worked":"Pure-prose verbal supervision (zero numbers, zero equations, zero <think> blocks) appears to lift broad math accuracy by +30.6 points. Possibilities: (a) genuine routing improvement from concept-naming prose, (b) eval is easier than expected once the model can identify the math frame, (c) wrapper artifact.","next_focus":"URGENT triangulation before celebrating: (1) run anchor to rule out stylistic clobber, (2) run numeric-drill-control (32 worked numerical examples on same domains) to test whether prose specifically was doing causal work vs just SFT. If anchor drops or control matches, demote this result.","what_failed":""},"notes":"ANCHOR: iter 1 adapter scored 0.935 (n=31) vs anchor baseline 1.000 (n=31). Regression of -0.065 / -6.5pp (2 items lost). Not Pyrrhic but worth fixing; iter 3 plan: lower-rank refinement (rank 2). The earlier metadata leak's 0.909/n=22 was a pilot, not the anchor — disregard it. Also overrides previous note that anchor baseline was 0.909."}
{"iter":2,"slug":"numeric-drill-control","ts":"2026-05-15T07:44:32Z","status":"discard","score":0.806452,"n":62,"delta":-0.096774,"hypothesis":"32 worked **numerical** examples covering the same 7 math domains as","dataset":{"path":"datasets/numeric-drill-control.jsonl","size":32},"training":{"adapter":"cap-numeric-drill-control","final_loss":0.242,"elapsed_s":40},"asi":{"what_worked":"Triangulation succeeded. Numeric drill on the same domains lifts +0.21 over baseline — confirming SFT on math helps in general — but falls -0.097 short of prose (iter 1). The prose route really does have an edge over surface-form-matching numeric drill, supporting the §4 T-family hypothesis.","next_focus":"iter 3: mixed-prose-numeric (64 prose + 32 numeric = 96 examples). Tests additivity: do the two routes stack to lift past 0.903, or does mixing wash out the prose advantage?","what_failed":""},"notes":"Note: training loss for this control was 0.24 vs iter 1's 1.58 — 6.5x lower loss but lower capability score. Strong reminder that final_loss does not predict eval accuracy (§16 loss-chasing anti-pattern)."}
