{"iter":10,"slug":"prose-mistake-named","ts":"2026-05-15T09:58:23Z","status":"discard","score":0.725807,"n":62,"delta":-0.225806,"hypothesis":"32 examples in which the assistant *names the typical novice mistake*","dataset":{"path":"datasets/prose-mistake-named.jsonl","size":32},"training":{"adapter":"cap-prose-mistake-named","final_loss":1.206,"elapsed_s":51},"asi":{"next_focus":"iter 11 meta-question (§15 says meta-questions transfer surprisingly well — last T-variant to test).","what_failed":"N-family (mistake-naming) clearly worse than T-family. Δ vs iter 1: -0.178. Likely failure: model learned to produce mistake-descriptions rather than terminal answers. Retire N-family entirely. Smaller dataset (32 vs 64) may have contributed but the regression magnitude points to a route failure, not dataset-size issue."},"notes":""}
{"iter":11,"slug":"meta-what-kind-of-problem","ts":"2026-05-15T10:08:35Z","status":"discard","score":0.919355,"n":62,"delta":-0.032258,"hypothesis":"32 examples where the user asks \"what kind of math problem is this?\"","dataset":{"path":"datasets/meta-what-kind-of-problem.jsonl","size":32},"training":{"adapter":"cap-meta-what-kind-of-problem","final_loss":1.506,"elapsed_s":49},"asi":{"what_worked":"Meta-questions ARE a viable T-route. 32 meta examples beat 64 prose-only examples (iter 1: 0.903 vs iter 11: 0.919). Per-example efficiency: meta is ~2x more efficient by examples. Validates §15 'meta-questions transfer surprisingly well'. But below iter 7's 0.952.","next_focus":"iter 12: mixed-meta-numeric per pre-committed plan. Test if meta + numeric anchor matches or beats iter 3.","what_failed":"Doesn't beat iter 3's 0.935 or iter 7's 0.952. Pure meta-routing alone isn't the best."},"notes":""}
